From ff1fd3fc19bc5c361950e58e7d14399b8b6fdaf4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Oleg Tolmatcev <oleg.tolmatcev@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 21:11:37 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] env: run #! scripts through their interpreter on Windows

Windows cannot execute scripts itself, so `env VAR=x ./script` failed
with "not a valid Win32 application" (os error 193); libtool runs its
helper scripts that way.

When the program cannot be started, read its #! line (the program is a
path if it has a directory separator, else it is looked up in PATH) and
run the interpreter with the script and the arguments. POSIX
interpreter paths such as /bin/sh do not exist on Windows, so the
interpreter is looked up by file name in PATH; `#!/usr/bin/env NAME`
names it in its argument. This matches brush's handling.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5-5
